ta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(1)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(2\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=2\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542376 1080 "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542376 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542376 1083 #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542377 1080 "\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\pi" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542377 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542377 1083 #uuid "3112b487-c350-57db-82e9-a721ed41fe97" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542371 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542378 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=9-2\\cdot y.$" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542378 1072 #uuid "3d825389-7a3e-5461-af4a-30fb0473dbe9" 13194139535921 17592186045443 true] [17592186542378 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bo